🐧 TryHackMe — Linux Fundamentals (Part 2 & 3)
📚 Notes + Real-World Applications
📌 PART 2 — System Basics & Administration
🔐 SSH (Remote Access)
ssh user@ip
💡 Real-world use:
Connect to remote Linux servers (AWS EC2, VPS, cloud VMs)
Server administration in production
Debugging live systems
⚙️ Example:
ssh ubuntu@54.23.xxx.xxx

👉 In real companies, most Linux servers are accessed this way.

📂 Linux File System Navigation
pwd
ls -lh
cd /etc
💡 Real-world meaning:

Linux is a file-based system, everything is organized as files.

Important directories:

/etc → system configuration (SSH, nginx, users)
/var/log → system logs
/home → user directories
/usr → installed applications

👉 In cloud environments, most debugging starts in /var/log.

📄 File Management
touch file.txt
cp file1 file2
mv file1 /tmp/
rm file.txt
💡 Real-world use:
Moving configuration files
Managing deployment scripts
Handling logs and backups
🔍 Searching Files
find / -name "config.yaml"
💡 Real-world use:
Finding lost configuration files
Searching secrets or API keys
Security audits
🔐 Linux Permissions (VERY IMPORTANT)
ls -l
chmod 755 script.sh

Example:

-rwxr-xr--
Meaning:
r = read
w = write
x = execute
💡 Real-world use:
Server security control
Protecting sensitive files (SSH keys, configs)
Web server access rules

👉 Example:

Private SSH key → chmod 600
Deploy script → chmod +x
👤 Users & Privileges
whoami
sudo -l
su root
💡 Real-world use:
Managing access control
Principle of least privilege (cybersecurity)
Privilege escalation testing (pentesting)

👉 In production: users are NEVER root by default.

📌 PART 3 — System Tools & Automation
✍️ Text Editors (nano / vim)
nano file.txt
vim file.txt
💡 Real-world use:
Editing server configs (nginx, apache, ssh)
Quick fixes on remote machines
DevOps debugging via SSH

👉 nano = simple
👉 vim = professional / sysadmin standard

🌐 Downloading & APIs
wget url
curl url
💡 Real-world use:
Download scripts or installers
Test APIs
Debug backend services

Example:

curl https://api.example.com/status
🔎 Searching Inside Files (grep)
grep "error" /var/log/syslog
💡 Real-world use:
Debug production issues
Analyze logs
Find failures in applications

👉 One of the MOST used commands in production systems.

⚙️ Processes Management
ps aux
top
kill PID
kill -9 PID
💡 Real-world use:
Identify CPU/memory-heavy processes
Kill stuck services
Debug server crashes
⏱️ Automation (Cron Jobs)
crontab -e

Example:

0 2 * * * /home/user/backup.sh
💡 Real-world use:
Automated backups
Log cleanup
Scheduled scripts (DevOps workflows)

👉 Core concept in DevOps automation.

📦 Package Management (APT)
sudo apt update
sudo apt install nginx
💡 Real-world use:
Installing servers (web, database, tools)
Security updates / patches
Setting up environments
📜 Logs (Critical for Debugging)
/var/log/syslog
tail -f /var/log/syslog
💡 Real-world use:
Debug server crashes
Monitor applications
Security incident investigation

Examples:

Website down → check nginx logs
App crash → backend logs
Intrusion detection → auth logs
